infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title The depth wall: non-tail vs tail recursion
  desc Same recursive type, two call positions — where the self-call sits decides whether you hit ~50 or ~1000
  compares
    - label Non-tail call
      children
        - label Self-call wrapped in a tuple
          desc [unknown, ...Build<N>] — each frame must wait for the inner result before it can build its own
        - label Walls out around 50 levels
          desc The compiler bounds nesting to keep checking from hanging; deeper than ~50 frames it gives up
        - label Errors: excessively deep
          desc Type instantiation is excessively deep and possibly infinite — the same message also flags a wrong base case
        - label Elimination cannot apply
          desc The wrapping tuple keeps every frame on the stack, so TS 4.5 has no frame to reuse
    - label Tail call (TS 4.5)
      children
        - label Whole branch IS the self-call
          desc Build<N, [unknown, ...Acc]> — nothing wraps it, so the branch result is the recursion itself
        - label Limit jumps to ~1000 levels
          desc Tail-recursion elimination reuses the evaluation frame instead of nesting — the wall moves ~20x
        - label Thread an accumulator
          desc Carry results in an Acc param and return it at the base case instead of rebuilding on the unwind
        - label Build<999> type-checks
          desc Build<999>["length"] resolves to 999 with no error — proof the wall lifted
theme
  palette #f43f5e #14b8a6
